@@ -89,6 +91,44 @@ app.get("/error", (event) => {
 > [!TIP]
 > For enhanced security, H3 hides certain fields of unhandled errors (`data`, `body`, `stack` and `message`) in JSON response.
 
+## Problem Details
+
+By default, error responses are JSON with `status`, `statusText`, `message`, `unhandled`, `data` and `body` fields. Enable the [`problemDetails`](/guide/api/h3) option to render them as [RFC 9457](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457) problem details with `Content-Type: application/problem+json`:
+
+```js
+const app = new H3({ problemDetails: true });
+
+app.get("/orders/:id", () => {
+  throw new HTTPError({
+    status: 422,
+    statusText: "Unprocessable Content",
+    message: "Invalid order",
+    type: "https://example.com/errors/validation",
+    instance: "/orders/42",
+    data: { field: "email" },
+  });
+});
+```
+
+Response body:
+
+```json
+{
+  "type": "https://example.com/errors/validation",
+  "title": "Unprocessable Content",
+  "status": 422,
+  "detail": "Invalid order",
+  "instance": "/orders/42",
+  "data": { "field": "email" }
+}
+```
+
+- `statusText` becomes `title` (omitted when not set) and `message` becomes `detail`.
+- `type` defaults to `about:blank` and `instance` is omitted when not set.
+- `data` and `body` keys are kept as extensions. RFC members win over colliding `body` keys.
+- `unhandled` is not part of this format. Unhandled errors keep their hidden `detail` (`"HTTPError"`) and omit `data` and `body`.
+- `headers` from `HTTPError` are still applied. Setting a `content-type` header there overrides `application/problem+json`.
+
 ## Catching Errors
 
 Using global [`onError`](/guide/api/h3#global-hooks) hook:
